  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The Market hosts Kestrel in-process. The Web SDK is deliberately not used: it would change
         this console server's output type and static-web-asset handling.
         THE RUNTIME IMAGE MUST MATCH - the root Dockerfile is pinned to dotnet/aspnet for this. -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <!-- DATAS (Dynamic Adaptation To Application Sizes) is ON here, which is also the .NET 9+
         default under Server GC. It was deliberately turned OFF on 2026-09-05 (#919) and that
         build reached prod on 2026-09-06; this line puts it back, on the repo owner's call after
         a live session on the DATAS-off build felt worse.

         Keep the measurements, because the revert is a trade and not a refutation. Prometheus,
         env=prod, the hour before the 19:34 UTC container recreate against the 70 minutes after:

           time_in_gc_ratio            0.447    ->  0.000
           GC pause, ms per second     5.82     ->  0.76
           gen0 collections per second 0.116    ->  0.010
           gen0 budget                 64 MB    ->  2392 MB
           tick p50                    0.95 ms  ->  0.68 ms
           tick p99, mean of samples   17.3 ms  ->  27.9 ms
           tick p99, max of samples    81.5 ms  ->  81.9 ms

         Total GC cost fell hard and p50 improved, but per-collection pause ROSE: 5.82/0.116 is
         ~50 ms per collection before, 0.756/0.010 is ~76 ms after (the gen0 counter increments on
         every GC, so it stands in for total collections; gen2 rate was 0.001 on both sides).
         DATAS-off trades many small pauses for a few large ones, and on a 16.7 ms frame budget it
         is the large ones a player feels. The tick p99 that #919 set out to fix moved the wrong
         way, which is the whole reason this is being put back.

         State the weakness of that too, so the next session does not re-litigate it on half the
         picture. The two windows are not load-matched - 46 players and 198 landblocks before
         against 35 and 136 after - and prod at the same hour the previous day, at the same 35
         players with DATAS still on, showed a WORSE p99 mean of 32.2 ms. So the p99 case for
         reverting is suggestive rather than settled; the per-collection pause case is the solid
         half of it.

         If DATAS-off is tried again, bound the pause instead of removing the mechanism. Raise
         DOTNET_GCDTargetTCP above its 2% default, or cap the gen0 budget with DOTNET_GCgen0size
         rather than letting it run out to 2.4 GB, and judge it on per-collection pause, not on
         total time in GC. -->
    <!-- MSBuild property / env var / runtimeconfig key are three different spellings of one
         setting - GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode here, DOTNET_GCDynamicAdaptationMode in the
         deploy compose files, System.GC.DynamicAdaptationMode in the generated runtimeconfig.json.
         All three take 1 enabled / 0 disabled. Verify the generated file after changing this,
         because a typo in an MSBuild property is silently ignored rather than failing the build.
         Note that stage cannot validate any change here: at 2-3 players its pauses are already
         ~0 ms, so the signal only exists on prod's dashboard. -->
    <GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>1</GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <RetainVMGarbageCollection>true</RetainVMGarbageCollection>
  </PropertyGroup>

